"Singer India Ltd. v. Chander Mohan Chadha And Others | Supreme Court Of India | Judgment | Law | CaseMine (https://www.casemine.com/judgement/in/5609ae0ce4b0149711412cd4)\nciteturn67search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 22.2 years ago; Crawled: 3 days ago; 15), Block ‘C’, Connaught Place, New Delhi was let out on 11-7-1966 by the landlord — Shri Atma Ram Chadha (whose legal representatives are Respondents 1-13) — to Singer Sewing Machine Company, an American corporation (“the American Company”) at a monthly rent of Rs 1200. ... Implications: The Supreme Court re-affirms that tenancy rights transferred by virtue of a court-sanctioned amalgamation constitute an assignment or parting with possession under Section 14(1)(b) of the Delhi Rent Control Act when effected without the landlord’s written consent.\n## Factual and Procedural Background\n\nShop No. 13/14 (Private No. 15), Block ‘C’, Connaught Place, New Delhi was let out on 11-7-1966 by the landlord — Shri Atma Ram Chadha (whose legal representatives are Respondents 1-13) — to Singer Sewing Machine Company, an American corporation (“the American Company”) at a monthly rent of Rs 1200.\n\nFollowing Reserve Bank of India directions issued under Section 29 of the Foreign Exchange Regulation Act, 1973 (FERA), the American Company promoted amalgamation proceedings. By order dated 31-12-1981 the Bombay High Court, acting under Sections 391 and 394 of the Companies Act, sanctioned a Scheme of Amalgamation whereby the entire Indian undertaking of the American Company, “including leases, rights of tenancy or occupancy”, vested in Indian Sewing Machine Company Limited (“the Indian Company”). The Indian Company subsequently changed its name to Singer India Limited, the present appellant.\n\nIn 1982 the landlord instituted eviction proceedings under Section 14(1)(b) of the Delhi Rent Control Act, 1958 (“the Act”) alleging unauthorised sub-letting/assignment. \nImplications: The Supreme Court re-affirms that tenancy rights transferred by virtue of a court-sanctioned amalgamation constitute an assignment or parting with possession under Section 14(1)(b) of the Delhi Rent Control Act when effected without the landlord’s written consent. Compliance with foreign-exchange regulations or other external compulsions does not create an exception, and tenants cannot rely on corporate-veil arguments to circumvent eviction. No new doctrine is announced, but the decision consolidates existing jurisprudence and serves as clear guidance for future landlord-tenant disputes involving corporate restructuring.\n\n## Singer India Ltd. v. Chander Mohan Chadha And Others\n\n[Input]\n\nG.P Mathur, J. This appeal, by special leave, has been preferred against the judgment and order dated 25-5-2001 of the Delhi High Court whereby the second appeal preferred by the appellant was dismissed and the judgment and order dated 8-4-1996 of the Rent Control Tribunal directing eviction of the appellant from the premises in dispute was affirmed.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDelhi Rent Act | Tenancy Transfer Without Landlord's Consent Attracts Eviction : Supreme Court Orders Eviction Of Merged Bank (https://www.livelaw.in/supreme-court/delhi-rent-act-tenancy-transfer-without-landlords-consent-attracts-eviction-supreme-court-orders-eviction-of-merged-bank-540649)\nciteturn67search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; ## The Court ordered the eviction of Punjab National Bank from a prime property in Delhi, as it got possesion after merger of original tenant-bank. ... Under the latter, a scheme becomes effective only after being placed before Parliament and undergoing potential modifications, making it legislative in character - a scheme under Section 45 of the Banking Regulation Act is merely placed before Parliament and remains administrative - judicial precedents governing legislative bank mergers do not apply to schemes under Section 45 of the Banking Regulation Act - Held: The components of Section 14(1)(b) of the Delhi Rent Control Act, 1958 stood fully satisfied as the original tenant parted with possession and ceased to exist, and PNB occupied the premises without the written consent of the landlord - The judgment of the High Court setting aside the eviction decree was set aside, and the eviction decree passed by the Additional Rent Control Tribunal was restored.\n\nThe landlord challenged the High Court's decision before the Supreme Court.\n\nSetting aside the impugned High Court judgment, the judgment authored by Justice Karol referred to Section 14(1)(b) of the Delhi Rent Control Act, stating that the provision is attracted once the tenant has sublet, assigned, or otherwise parted with possession without obtaining the landlord's written consent.\n\n“…where, upon amalgamation effected under Section 45 of the Banking Regulation Act, the tenancy rights vest in another entity and possession qua tenanted premises passes to it without the written consent of the landlord, the ingredients of Section 14(1)(b) shall stand fully satisfied. The reasons necessitating such transfer or whether it was voluntary or involuntary, are wholly immaterial for the purposes of attracting the said provision.”, the Court observed, relying on Bhairon Sahai v. Bishamber Dayal, (2017) 8 SCC 492.\n\nResultantly, the appeal was allowed, and the Respondent-PNB was ordered to evict the premises by January 31, 2027.\n\nHeadnote\n\nDelhi Rent Control Act, 1958 – Section 14(1)(b) – Protection of tenant against eviction – Sub-letting, assignment, or parting with possession without written consent of the landlord – Statutory Amalgamation of Banks – Banking Regulation Act, 1949 – Section 45 – i. Factum of Parting with Possession via Amalgamation: The appellant-landlord leased premises to Hindustan Commercial Bank (HCB) - a Gazette Notification issued under Section 45(7) of the Banking Regulation Act, 1949, HCB was amalgamated with the Punjab National Bank (PNB), whereby the original tenant (HCB) ceased to exist and all its rights, liabilities, and assets including the tenancy rights vested in PNB without the written consent of the landlord; ii. Irrelevance of Voluntary vs. Involuntary Transfer: Section 14(1)(b) of the Delhi Rent Control Act, 1958 is of wide amplitude and encompasses every mode by which possession or tenancy rights are transferred from the original tenant to another entity - The provision does not draw any distinction between voluntary and involuntary transfers, nor does it carve out an exception for transfers executed pursuant to a scheme of amalgamation or to secure compliance with law - The reasons necessitating such a transfer are wholly immaterial; iv. Nature of Amalgamation Scheme under the Banking Regulation Act: The scheme-making process under Section 45 of the Banking Regulation Act, 1949 is strictly administrative in nature and not legislative - Merely because a scheme framed by the Reserve Bank of India (RBI) is sanctioned by the Central Government and placed before both Houses of Parliament does not elevate it to a statutory enactment capable of overriding the non-obstante provisions of Section 14(1)(b) of the Delhi Rent Control Act, 1958. [Paras 11-18]\n\nDistinction from the Banking Companies (Acquisition and Transfer of Undertakings) Act, 1980: An essential distinction exists between Section 45 of the Banking Regulation Act, 1949 and Section 9 of the Acquisition Act, 1980. Under the latter, a scheme becomes effective only after being placed before Parliament and undergoing potential modifications, making it legislative in character - a scheme under Section 45 of the Banking Regulation Act is merely placed before Parliament and remains administrative - judicial precedents governing legislative bank mergers do not apply to schemes under Section 45 of the Banking Regulation Act - Held: The components of Section 14(1)(b) of the Delhi Rent Control Act, 1958 stood fully satisfied as the original tenant parted with possession and ceased to exist, and PNB occupied the premises without the written consent of the landlord - The judgment of the High Court setting aside the eviction decree was set aside, and the eviction decree passed by the Additional Rent Control Tribunal was restored. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nM/s. Singer India Limited vs Shri Atma Ram Chadha, landlord - 2004 Supreme(Online)(SC) 146 (https://supremetoday.ai/doc/judgement/INDSC00000058826)\nciteturn67search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; ## Headnote:(A) Delhi Rent Control Act, 1958 - Section 14(1)(b) - Eviction of tenant for subletting - Tenant's argument regarding amalgamation of companies and parting of possession not exempting it from eviction - Section clearly applicable irrespective of voluntary or involuntary transfers - Court emphasized that the corporate identity merger does not change nature of tenancy rights as per law. ... In the year 1982, the landlord filed an eviction petition on the ground, inter alia, that the American Company, without obtaining any written consent from the landlord, had parted with the possession of the premises in dispute in favour of Indian Sewing Machine Company Limited, incorporated under the Indian Companies Act (hereinafter referred to as 'Indian Company'), and it was the said company which was in exclusive possession of the premises and thereby it was liable for eviction in view of S.14(1)(b) of the Delhi Rent Control Act (hereinafter referred to as the 'Act').\n\n### 2004 Supreme(Online)(SC) 146\n\nSUPREME COURT\nR.V. Raveendran, L. S. Panta, JJ\nM/s. Singer India Limited – Appellant\nVersus\nShri Atma Ram Chadha, landlord – Respondent\nCivil Appeal No. 387 of 2004 | Civil Appeal No. 388 of 2004\n\nAdvocates:\nFor the Appellants/Petitioners: Shri Anil Divan\nFor the Respondents: Shri Dushyant Dave\n\n# Amalgamation does not exempt tenants from eviction under subletting provisions of the Delhi Rent Control Act.\n\n## Headnote:(A) Delhi Rent Control Act, 1958 - Section 14(1)(b) - Eviction of tenant for subletting - Tenant's argument regarding amalgamation of companies and parting of possession not exempting it from eviction - Section clearly applicable irrespective of voluntary or involuntary transfers - Court emphasized that the corporate identity merger does not change nature of tenancy rights as per law. (Paras 4, 5, 11, 12)\n(B) Amalgamation of Companies - Legal principles - The transfer of lease rights during amalgamation considered a form of parting with possession - No statutory protection exists for the appellant in escaping eviction. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIcici Bank Limited v. Late Smt. Shakuntla Gupta (Since Deceased) Represented Through: Lrs | Delhi High Court | Judgment | Law | CaseMine (https://www.casemine.com/judgement/in/56b49375607dba348f006ff9)\nciteturn67search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.2 years ago; Crawled: 2 days ago; It would be enough for us to highlight that the decision of the Supreme Court in Singer's case (supra) concerned the ejectment under Section 14(1)(b) of Delhi Rent Control Act, 1957 where as a result of amalgamation the tenanted premises held by the transferor company came to the transferee company, and the Supreme Court held that it would be a case of assignment and since the assignment, as in the instant case, was without the written consent of the landlord, it was held that Singer (transferee company) was liable for ejectment.\nSuch a matter has to be examined and decided in accordance with the special jurisdiction created by that Act or on a regular civil action, if maintainable. No cause of action accrues to the landlord before the amalgamation and consequential vesting. The cause of action, if any, follows the amalgamation and the vesting. Neither the amalgamation nor the vesting would deprive the landlord of any plea based on alleged assignment which may be open in law to the landlord. If there is any assignment in law, which may attract the provisions of the Delhi Rent Control Act, the landlord would be free to take recourse to the proceedings under that Act or in a regular civil action and such proceedings would be dealt with and decided by the appropriate authority in accordance with law.”\n\n21. It would be enough for us to highlight that the decision of the Supreme Court in Singer's case (supra) concerned the ejectment under Section 14(1)(b) of Delhi Rent Control Act, 1957 where as a result of amalgamation the tenanted premises held by the transferor company came to the transferee company, and the Supreme Court held that it would be a case of assignment and since the assignment, as in the instant case, was without the written consent of the landlord, it was held that Singer (transferee company) was liable for ejectment.\n\n22. The two contentions advanced before the learned Single Judge having been dealt with by us and the conclusions arrived at by the learned Single Judge, being affirmed, we deal with the contention advanced that even if there was a violation of clause 10 of the lease-deed the lease could not be determined because a term of the lease was violated and not a condition thereof.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAsha Rohtagi & Ors v. Erstwhile New Bank Of India Through General Mananger Pnb | Delhi High Court | Judgment | Law | CaseMine (https://www.casemine.com/judgement/in/56090bc2e4b01497111755cf)\nciteturn67search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 21.5 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; The petitioner (landlord) filed an eviction petition on 3.10.1996 under section 14(1)(a) and (b) of the Delhi Rent Control Act seeking eviction of New Bank of India (NBI) through its General Manager and Punjab National Bank (PNB) from commercial premises K-16, Chaudhary Building, Connaught Circus, New Delhi. ... On the section 14(1)(b) (subletting/assignment) ground, ARC held that the statutory merger/amalgamation under the 4.9.1993 notification amounted to subletting/assignment and granted eviction.\n# Structured Summary of the Opinion (O.P. Dwivedi, J.)\n\n## Factual and Procedural Background\n\nThe petitioner (landlord) filed an eviction petition on 3.10.1996 under section 14(1)(a) and (b) of the Delhi Rent Control Act seeking eviction of New Bank of India (NBI) through its General Manager and Punjab National Bank (PNB) from commercial premises K-16, Chaudhary Building, Connaught Circus, New Delhi. The landlord alleged non-payment of rent (arrears from October 1993 onwards despite a demand notice dated 10.10.1994) and that the tenant (NBI) had sublet, assigned or parted with possession to PNB without written consent. Respondent Nos. 2 and 3 (officers of PNB) denied the claim, contending that NBI ceased to exist having been amalgamated into PNB under a Government notification dated 4.9.1993 issued under section 9 of the Banking Companies (Acquisition and Transfer of Undertaking) Act, 1980, which, they said, conferred tenancy rights on PNB as statutory successor. There was no appearance for respondent No. 1 (NBI).\n\nThe Additional Rent Controller (ARC) by order dated 24.11.2004 found that the demand notice was served and there was default in payment of rent; however, ARC concluded there was no landlord-tenant relationship between the petitioner and PNB because NBI had ceased to exist, and therefore dismissed the petition on the section 14(1)(a) (non-payment) ground. On the section 14(1)(b) (subletting/assignment) ground, ARC held that the statutory merger/amalgamation under the 4.9.1993 notification amounted to subletting/assignment and granted eviction.\n\nThe respondents appealed to the Rent Control Tribunal (RCA No. 609/2004), which set aside the ARC order, holding that the statutory scheme promulgated by the Government dated 4.9.1993 conferring tenancy rights on PNB was legislative in character and did not amount to subletting, assignment or parting with possession within the meaning of section 14(1)(b). The landlord thereafter invoked Article 227 of the Constitution of India before the High Court challenging the Tribunal's order.\n\n## Legal Issues Presented\n\n  1. Whether vesting/conferment of tenancy rights on PNB by virtue of the Government notification dated 4.9.1993 issued under section 9 of the Banking Companies (Acquisition and Transfer of Undertaking) Act, 1980, amounts to \"sub-letting, assignment or parting with possession\" within the meaning of section 14(1)(b) of the Delhi Rent Control Act.\n  2. Whether a landlord-tenant relationship exists between the petitioner and PNB (i.e., whether PNB is a tenant for the purposes of examining the ground of non-payment of rent under section 14(1)(a)), and the consequences of the Tribunal's finding on that relationship for the earlier order dated 29.9.1997 under section 15(4) of the Act.\n\n## Arguments of the Parties\n\n### Petitioner's (Landlord's) Arguments\n\n  * The landlord filed for eviction on two grounds: (a) non-payment of rent — arrears from October 1993 despite a demand notice dated 10.10.1994; and (b) subletting/assignment/parting with possession under section 14(1)(b), alleging that NBI had parted with possession to PNB via respondents without the landlord's written consent.\n  * Implicit in the landlord's position is that any transfer resulting in PNB occupying the premises without the landlord's consent should be treated as subletting/assignment within the meaning of the Rent Act.\n\n### Respondents' (PNB and officers') Arguments\n\n  * Respondents denied the claim of arrears and the effect of the demand notice.\n  * They asserted that NBI had been amalgamated into PNB under the Government notification dated 4.9.1993 issued under section 9 of the Banking Companies (Acquisition and Transfer of Undertaking) Act, 1980, and that tenancy rights were conferred on PNB by statute; consequently PNB is a statutory successor and there was no subletting, assignment or parting with possession by the tenant.\nCox & Kings Agencies Ltd. v. Chander Malhotra ((1997) 2 SCC 687)  | Transfer of lease rights from a foreign company to an Indian company under compulsion (FERA-related) was analyzed; the Court found on facts it was a voluntary assignment and therefore could amount to subletting/assignment within Rent Act.  | The court relied on this case as part of the body of authority showing that transfers under Companies Act/contractual arrangements (even if prompted by external regulation) can be assignments; used here to contrast with the present statutory vesting under Banking Act notification.\nSinger India Ltd. v. Chander Mohan Chadha & Others ((2004) 7 SCC 1)  | Even where a Court-sanctioned scheme under sections 391/394 Companies Act transfers leases and tenancy rights, section 14(1)(b) of the Rent Act applies; the Act does not exempt a lessee who adopts amalgamation to comply with law.  | The court cited Singer as a clear authority that amalgamation under Companies Act leading to transfer of tenancy rights does not attract an exception from section 14(1)(b); the judgment helped the court distinguish company amalgamations from statutory vesting under a sovereign enactment.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSINGER INDIA LTD. versus CHANDER MOHAN CHADHA AND ORS. — 2004 INSC 447 - Legal Desk AI (https://www.legaldeskai.in/case-law/in/sc/judgment/singer-india-ltd-versus-chander-mohan-chadha-and-ors-2004-s-2004-3-535-552)\nciteturn67search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 22.2 years ago; Crawled: 3 days ago; Section 14(1)(b) of the Delhi Rent Control Act applies to any factual sub‑letting, assignment or parting with possession, including transfers effected by amalgamation, and the corporate veil cannot be lifted to avoid eviction. ... The landlord sued Singer India Ltd for eviction under Section 14(1)(b) of the Delhi Rent Control Act, alleging that the American tenant had transferred possession to the Indian company without the landlord's written consent.\n\nSupreme Court of India\n\n# SINGER INDIA LTD.versus CHANDER MOHAN CHADHA AND ORS.\n\nCitation\n    2004 INSC 447\n\nDecided\n    13 August 2004\n\nDisposal\n    Dismissed\n\nBench\n    R C LAHOTI\n\n## Holding\n\nSection 14(1)(b) of the Delhi Rent Control Act applies to any factual sub‑letting, assignment or parting with possession, including transfers effected by amalgamation, and the corporate veil cannot be lifted to avoid eviction.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe landlord sued Singer India Ltd for eviction under Section 14(1)(b) of the Delhi Rent Control Act, alleging that the American tenant had transferred possession to the Indian company without the landlord's written consent. The transfer resulted from a court‑sanctioned amalgamation compelled by a Reserve Bank of India direction under Section 29 of the Foreign Exchange Regulation Act. The Supreme Court held that the factual occurrence of sub‑letting, assignment or parting with possession triggers Section 14(1)(b) irrespective of whether the transfer was voluntary or compelled, and that an amalgamation does not create an exception to the provision. \n## Legislation cited\n\n  * Companies Act, 1956s. 391, s. 394\n  * Constitution of Indias. 136\n  * Delhi Rent Control Act, 1958s. 14(1)(b)\n  * Foreign Exchange Regulation Act, 1973s. 29\n\n## Subjects\n\nrent control eviction sub‑letting assignment parting with possession Delhi Rent Control Act Section 14(1)(b)amalgamation corporate veil Companies Act FERA Special Leave Petition\n    \n                        SINGER INDIA LTD.                                        A\n                                v.\n                 CHANDER MOHAN CHADHA AND ORS.\n    \n                              AUGUST 13, 2004\n    \n       [R.C. LAHOTI, CJ., G.P. MATHUR AND C.K. THAKKER,.JJ.]                     B\n    \n         Rent Control and Eviction:\n    \n         Delhi Rent Control Act, 1958:\n    \n          Section l 4(l)(b)-Subletting, assignment or parting with possession-\n                                                                                 c\n    Without written consent from landlord-Suit premises let out to an\n    American company-RBI directed reduction of equity capital of the said\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSinger India Limited vs Chander Mohan Chadha & Ors on 13 August, 2004 (https://indiankanoon.org/docfragment/1134266/?formInput=section+14++++++filter%3A++++++delhi+rent+control+act)\nciteturn67search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 22.2 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; Learned counsel has further submitted that in the peculiar facts of the present case, Section 14(1)(b) of the Delhi Rent Control Act should not be literally construed but a purposive construction should be given. ... Learned counsel has further submitted that as the Company Petition for sanctioning the scheme of amalgamation was filed in order to secure compliance with law, namely, to reduce the equity capital of the American Company to 40 per cent and as the \"lease, rights of tenancy or occupancy\" of the said company got vested with the Indian Company in view of the sanction granted by the Bombay High Court i.e. under the orders of the Court, the principle laid down by this Court in Hindustan Petroleum Corporation Ltd and another vs.Shyam Co-operative Housing Society and others 1988 (4) SCC 747 will be applicable and no order of eviction can be passed against the appellant.\n(b) of the Act would not be attracted. Learned counsel has further submitted that in the peculiar facts of the present case, Section 14(1)(b) of the Delhi Rent Control Act should not be literally construed but a purposive construction should be given. Reference in this connection has been made to a decision of Delhi High Court in Telesound India Ltd. In re. 1983 (53) Company Cases 926 wherein it has been held that the effect of an order of amalgamation passed under Section 394 of the Companies Act is that the rights, properties and the liabilities of the transferor company become the rights, property and liabilities of the transferee-company and it is neither an assignment of right or property nor an assignment of the property by the company. On amalgamation, the transferor-company merges into the transferee-company shedding its corporate shell, but for all practical purposes remaining alive and thriving as part of the larger whole. The transferor-company is dissolved not because it has died or ceased to exist, but because for all practical purposes it has merged into another corporate shell. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIN THE APPELLATE TRIBUNAL FOR ELECTRICITY (https://www.aptel.gov.in/old_website/judgements/Judgment%20in%20appeal%20no.%2060%20of%2006.pdf)\nciteturn67search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; In Singer India Ltd. ... 1, while considering the scope of Section 14 (1) proviso (b) of The Delhi Rent ... sanctioning the Scheme of Amalgamation under Sections 391 to 394 of ... insofar as the applicability of Sections 14(1)(b) is concerned, as the act ... “The main principle on which such a course of action can be taken was\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n* \nIN THE HIGH COURT OF DELHI AT NEW DELHI \n \n% (https://pdf.lawlens.in/c07915c1-8704-40f9-99a5-adabe90852ef)\nciteturn67search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; consent of the landlord would render the company or the transferee-company liable to eviction under Section 14(1)(b) of | --- | --- consent of the landlord would render the company or the the Rent Control Act or otherwise be actionable in a regular | consent of the landlord would render the company or the civil action against them. ... Neither the amalgamation nor | consent of the landlord would render the company or the the vesting would deprive the landlord of any plea based on | consent of the landlord would render the company or the alleged assignment which may be open in law to the landlord. ... Section 14(1)(b) of Delhi Rent Control Act, 1957 where as a result of\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nthough, as a matter of law, the transferee companies are independent corporate entities, equally, as a matter of business reality, the Court cannot ignore the plea of the Transferee Company that the health and the wellbeing of its wholly owned subsidiaries was a matter which was legitimately entitled to be taken into account by the Transferee Company in coming out with the decision to amalgamate its wholly owned subsidiaries with itself. In the circumstances, the plea that the scheme of Amalgamation is an attempt to reduce the business and operational losses, inclusive of manpower and machinery costs ought to be accepted. Similarly the foundation of the scheme for Amalgamation is that the amalgamation will enable the three companies to pool together human, material and financial resources. This consideration particularly in a case where the two Transferor Companies are wholly owned subsidiaries cannot be regarded as extraneous or irrelevant. This Court, taking into consideration the business reality noted that the holding company seeks to emerge from the economic difficulty which face its subsidiaries which have become loss making entities. The effort is to pool together human, financial and material resources and to deploy them, upon amalgamation in a manner that would enhance profitability. This is a permissible object and nothing in the proposed scheme in the present case militates against commercial morality, the public interest or a view which a reasonable body or shareholders or creditors would adopt. The impugned order cannot, in such circumstances, be held to be irrational or based on any extraneous or irrelevant considerations. (https://www.icsi.edu/WebModules/LinksOfWeeks/ICSI-Jan_2018.pdf)\nciteturn67search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.7 years ago; In the circumstances, the plea that the scheme of Amalgamation is an attempt to reduce the business and operational losses, inclusive of manpower and machinery costs ought to be accepted. ... THE ORIENTAL INSURANCE CO. ... NDMC Act, 1994 read with Delhi Rent Control Act, 1958 - collection of property tax as arrears of rent - non-payment of property tax by tenant - eviction sought by landlord under Rent Act - whether tenant could be evicted as failure to pay rent - Held, No.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSupreme Court Ruling on Lease Eviction | PDF | Leasehold Estate | Lease (https://www.scribd.com/document/762413037/Singer-India-Ltd-vs-Chander-Mohan-Chadha-and-Ors)\nciteturn67search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 22.2 years ago; Crawled: 2.1 years ago; The judicial reasoning concluded that the amalgamation effectively transferred lease rights from the American to the Indian Company, fitting the criteria of parting with possession as per Section 14(1)(b) of the Delhi Rent Control Act.Since the American Company lost its separate legal identity and the Indian Company occupied the premises, the original landlord-tenant relationship was disrupted, justifying eviction.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSubhash Chand Goel And Others v. Hans Raj Gupta & Co. Pvt. Ltd . . | Delhi High Court | Judgment | Law | CaseMine (https://www.casemine.com/judgement/in/5d73f7a2714d58309a547bf3/amp)\nciteturn67search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.1 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago;   * Reliance on Supreme Court judgments (General Radio, Cox & Kings, Singer India) that an amalgamation order results in transfer of tenancy and amounts to sub-letting under the Act. ... The holding affirms that amalgamation of tenant companies without landlord's c